UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 4, Lesson 3: Conjunctions and complex sentences', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Join ideas with because, when, although and if to build clear complex sentences.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Keep the independent response and two written check responses; record oral retelling or performance where required by the task.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Create a new example using a completely different character or setting, show the same skill, then explain to another person how you checked its accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"WE DO — attempt each prompt before comparing to the feedback.\n1. Which part can stand alone: \"Tara carried a torch because the cave was dark\"?\nCOMPARE: \"Tara carried a torch\" is independent; the because-clause depends on it.\n2. Join \"We packed jumpers\" and \"the evening grew cold\" with because.\nCOMPARE: \"We packed jumpers because the evening grew cold.\"\n3. Write one sentence with although about a picnic and wet grass.\nCOMPARE: Example: \"Although the grass was wet, we spread the blanket on the dry rise.\"\n4. Punctuate: When the tide rose we moved our towels.\nCOMPARE: \"When the tide rose, we moved our towels.\"","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"YOU DO — complete the following substantial original task, not a copied model.\nWrite four original complex sentences: one each with because, when, although and if. Mark the independent clause in each sentence and explain one punctuation choice.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Join ideas with because, when, although and if to build clear complex sentences.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and spelling","materials":["Paper or typing area","Pencil for optional evidence"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper to copy only the original independent prompt; have the child respond in writing or orally as required. Save a photograph or brief parent observation for the learning record.","outcome_codes":["EN2-CWT-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance: Require four different conjunctions, accurate clause marking and a brief punctuation reason. Accept varied topics. Accept plausible original solutions where the text supports them; require the learner to explain at least one independent choice.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: \"We moved inside\" can stand alone. \"because rain soaked the field\" cannot; it needs the main clause.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Using because for every relationship hides the real connection between ideas. Correction: Example: \"Although the grass was wet, we spread the blanket on the dry rise.\"\n\nParent: Assess comprehension and language rather than penalising speech or motor difficulties unrelated to the objective. Keep written models out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"An independent clause can stand alone as a complete sentence. A dependent clause needs that main clause to finish its meaning. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["An independent clause can stand alone as a complete sentence. A dependent clause needs that main clause to finish its meaning.","A capital letter alone does not make a dependent clause a sentence.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Find the independent clause\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Because introduces a reason that explains the main action. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Because introduces a reason that explains the main action.","Putting a full stop before a because-clause creates a fragment.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Use because for a reason\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"When often shows time; although shows contrast; if shows a condition. Choose the conjunction that matches the relationship. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["When often shows time; although shows contrast; if shows a condition. Choose the conjunction that matches the relationship.","Using because for every relationship hides the real connection between ideas.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Use when, although and if carefully\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"If the dependent clause comes first, a comma often follows it. Then reread to check both parts make sense together. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["If the dependent clause comes first, a comma often follows it. Then reread to check both parts make sense together.","Do not add a comma that splits a short subject from its verb.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Punctuate and reread the whole sentence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"\"Tara carried a torch\" is independent; the because-clause depends on it. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["\"Tara carried a torch\" is independent; the because-clause depends on it.","A capital letter alone does not make a dependent clause a sentence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Which part can stand alone: \"Tara carried a torch because the cave was dark\"?\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"\"We packed jumpers because the evening grew cold.\" This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["\"We packed jumpers because the evening grew cold.\"","Putting a full stop before a because-clause creates a fragment.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Join \"We packed jumpers\" and \"the evening grew cold\" with because.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Example: \"Although the grass was wet, we spread the blanket on the dry rise.\" This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["Example: \"Although the grass was wet, we spread the blanket on the dry rise.\"","Using because for every relationship hides the real connection between ideas.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Write one sentence with although about a picnic and wet grass.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"\"When the tide rose, we moved our towels.\" This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["\"When the tide rose, we moved our towels.\"","Do not add a comma that splits a short subject from its verb.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Punctuate: When the tide rose we moved our towels.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Meaningful understanding is shown when a learner transfers the method to new text or speech and explains their evidence.","marking_guide":"Require an original relevant example, accurate terminology and an explanation grounded in the example.","question":"Use your own example to demonstrate independent and explain the evidence supporting your choice.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: \"We moved inside\" can stand alone. \"because rain soaked the field\" cannot; it needs the main clause.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"Explaining a correction helps the learner distinguish an effective strategy from a plausible-sounding but inaccurate approach.","marking_guide":"Require a genuine relevant error, accurate correction and a reason that refers to the target concept.","question":"Identify and correct a common mistake relating to conjunction in this lesson.","sample_answer":"Common error: Using because for every relationship hides the real connection between ideas. Correction: Example: \"Although the grass was wet, we spread the blanket on the dry rise.\"","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Submit your original independent work and your brief explanation of the key choices you made.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w04-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; compare the existing Coming Soon card and official syllabus content points before marking verified.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise independent.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Your goal is: Join ideas with because, when, although and if to build clear complex sentences."],"guided":["Attempt each guided prompt without looking ahead.","Compare your attempt with the supplied specific feedback.","Guided comparison: \"We packed jumpers because the evening grew cold.\""],"learn":["Study the first worked demonstration carefully.","Name the exact words or events that make it work.","Demonstration: \"We moved inside\" can stand alone. \"because rain soaked the field\" cannot; it needs the main clause."],"quiz":["Read the whole question and eliminate misconceptions.","Prefer the option that quotes or uses actual evidence.","Likely trap: Using because for every relationship hides the real connection between ideas."],"submit":["Include your own response and a short reason.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation before sending.","Success looks like: Join ideas with because, when, although and if to build clear complex sentences."],"teach0":["Focus on find the independent clause.","Hold this teaching point: An independent clause can stand alone as a complete sentence. A dependent clause needs that main clause to finish its me","Worked comparison: \"Tara carried a torch\" is independent; the because-clause depends on it."],"teach1":["Focus on use because for a reason.","Hold this teaching point: Because introduces a reason that explains the main action.","Worked comparison: \"We packed jumpers because the evening grew cold.\""],"teach2":["Focus on use when, although and if carefully.","Hold this teaching point: When often shows time; although shows contrast; if shows a condition. Choose the conjunction that matches the relationsh","Worked comparison: Example: \"Although the grass was wet, we spread the blanket on the dry rise.\""],"teach3":["Focus on punctuate and reread the whole sentence.","Hold this teaching point: If the dependent clause comes first, a comma often follows it. Then reread to check both parts make sense together.","Worked comparison: \"When the tide rose, we moved our towels.\""],"write":["Use the independent instructions, not the model words.","Give an original response and the reason behind it.","Independent reminder: Write four original complex sentences: one each with because, when, although and if. Mark the independent clause in each sentence and explain one punctuation ch"]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use independent with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify dependent and point to the words or events that support it.","I can practise conjunction independently on a new prompt.","I can notice clause needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying independent independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to find the independent clause?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"We moved inside\" can stand alone. \"because rain soaked the field\" cannot; it needs the main clause.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nWhich part can stand alone: \"Tara carried a torch because the cave was dark\"?\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\n\"Tara carried a torch\" is independent; the because-clause depends on it.","explain":"An independent clause can stand alone as a complete sentence. A dependent clause needs that main clause to finish its meaning.\n\nAn independent clause can stand alone as a complete sentence. A dependent clause needs that main clause to finish its meaning. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nA capital letter alone does not make a dependent clause a sentence. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Find the independent clause","visual":[{"alt":"Independent clause We moved inside beside dependent because-clause about rain.","caption":"The because-clause cannot stand alone as a complete sentence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Independent</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">We moved inside</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Dependent</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">because rain soaked the field</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I read the first example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or events that carry the meaning before you copy a conclusion.","who":"juno"},{"text":"So I should try the small prompt myself, then compare it with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"We moved inside\" can stand alone. \"because rain soaked the field\" cannot; it needs the main clause. Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I read the first example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or events that carry the meaning before you copy a conclusion.\nPip: So I should try the small prompt myself, then compare it with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"We moved inside\" can stand alone. \"because rain soaked the field\" cannot; it needs the main clause. Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying dependent independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to use because for a reason?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"Tara carried a torch because the cave was dark.\" The reason follows because.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nJoin \"We packed jumpers\" and \"the evening grew cold\" with because.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\n\"We packed jumpers because the evening grew cold.\"","explain":"Because introduces a reason that explains the main action.\n\nBecause introduces a reason that explains the main action. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nPutting a full stop before a because-clause creates a fragment. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Use because for a reason","visual":[{"alt":"Because labelled as reason beside although labelled as contrast.","caption":"Choose the conjunction that matches the relationship between ideas.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">because</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">gives a reason</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">although</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">shows a contrast</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying conjunction independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to use when, although and if carefully?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"When the bell rang, we lined up.\" Time. \"Although the path was muddy, we kept walking.\" Contrast. \"If it rains, we will read inside.\" Condition.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nWrite one sentence with although about a picnic and wet grass.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nExample: \"Although the grass was wet, we spread the blanket on the dry rise.\"","explain":"When often shows time; although shows contrast; if shows a condition. Choose the conjunction that matches the relationship.\n\nWhen often shows time; although shows contrast; if shows a condition. Choose the conjunction that matches the relationship. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nUsing because for every relationship hides the real connection between ideas. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Use when, although and if carefully"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying clause independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to punctuate and reread the whole sentence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"Because the cave was dark, Tara carried a torch.\" The comma follows the opening clause.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nPunctuate: When the tide rose we moved our towels.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\n\"When the tide rose, we moved our towels.\"","explain":"If the dependent clause comes first, a comma often follows it. Then reread to check both parts make sense together.\n\nIf the dependent clause comes first, a comma often follows it. Then reread to check both parts make sense together. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nDo not add a comma that splits a short subject from its verb. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Punctuate and reread the whole sentence"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 4, Lesson 3: Conjunctions and complex sentences","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative: story elements and planning","week":4,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"I DO — \"We moved inside\" can stand alone. \"because rain soaked the field\" cannot; it needs the main clause.\n\nNow look for the exact evidence in the words or actions. \"Tara carried a torch because the cave was dark.\" The reason follows because.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Independent clause We moved inside beside dependent because-clause about rain.","caption":"The because-clause cannot stand alone as a complete sentence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Independent</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">We moved inside</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Dependent</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">because rain soaked the field</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Because labelled as reason beside although labelled as contrast.","caption":"Choose the conjunction that matches the relationship between ideas.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\